5	\section{Declarations with Static Parameters}
6	\seclabel{overloading-static-params}
7	
8	The rules above compare the parameter types and the return types of
9	declarations without static parameters.
10	A declaration with static parameters is read as one declaration whose parameter
11	type ranges over every instance of it whose static arguments satisfy their
12	bounds (\secref{overloading-terms}).
13	A declaration $d_1$ is more specific than a declaration $d_2$ when $d_2$ is
14	applicable to every call to which $d_1$ is applicable, and not the other way
15	round (\secref{resolving-overloading}); where neither has static parameters,
16	this is the relation $\Ps \strictsubtype \Qs$ of the rules above.
17	A set of overloaded declarations, some or all of which have static parameters,
18	is a valid overloading when every pair $d_1$, $d_2$ of them satisfies the
19	following three rules:
20	\begin{itemize}
21	\item
22	The \emph{No Duplicates Rule}: $d_1$ and $d_2$ are not each applicable to every
23	call to which the other is applicable.
24	\item
25	The \emph{Meet Rule}: $d_1$ and $d_2$ exclude each other, or one of them is more
26	specific than the other, or some declaration of the set is more specific than
27	both and applicable to every call to which both are applicable.
28	\item
29	The \emph{Return Type Rule}: if $d_1$ is more specific than $d_2$, then for
30	every argument type other than \TYP{BottomType} to which $d_1$ is applicable,
31	and for every instance of $d_2$ that is applicable to it, some instance of $d_1$
32	is applicable to it whose return type is a subtype of the return type of that
33	instance of $d_2$.
34	\end{itemize}
35	\revision{revival-overloading}{The revival's text of 30~September 2026 stated
36	the No Duplicates Rule as ``$d_1$ and $d_2$ are not each more specific than the
37	other'', which the strict sense of more specific above made refuse no pair.
38	It is stated on the relation without ``and not the other way round'', as the
39	type group's papers of 2011 and 2019 state it, and so it refuses
40	\EXP{\VAR{dup}\llbracket{}T\rrbracket(x\COLON \TYP{Box}\llbracket{}T\rrbracket)}
41	beside \EXP{\VAR{dup}\llbracket{}U\rrbracket(x\COLON
42	\TYP{Box}\llbracket{}U\rrbracket)}, each applicable to every call to which the
43	other is.}
44	The Subtype Rule, the Incompatibility Rule and the Meet Rule above are these
45	rules for declarations without static parameters, with the conditions that
46	coercion adds; the Return Type Rule is the Subtype Rule's condition on return
47	types, checked over every instance of the less specific declaration and not
48	over one instance chosen for the pair.
49	
50	As in \secref{more-specific-rule}, declarations of the set that are each more
51	specific than both $d_1$ and $d_2$ and that together cover every argument to
52	which both are applicable also satisfy the Meet Rule.
53	\revision{revival-meet}{The type group's paper of 2011 states the Meet Rule
54	without this case.}
55	
56	Two parameter types with static parameters exclude each other, for the Meet
57	Rule and the Incompatibility Rule, as the type group's paper of 2011 decides it
58	by \emph{existential reduction}: each static parameter stands for any type
59	within its bound, and where the two parameter types mention one parameterized
60	trait, instantiation exclusion (\secref{trait-types}) lets them meet only where
61	the static arguments of those mentions are the same, every static argument but
62	an operator argument counting.
63	So where \TYP{Box} and \TYP{Pair} are distinct object types with static
64	parameters, the parameter types \EXP{\TYP{Box}\llbracket{}T\rrbracket} and
65	\EXP{\TYP{Pair}\llbracket{}A, B\rrbracket} of two declarations exclude each
66	other whatever \VAR{T}, \VAR{A} and \VAR{B} stand for.
67	
68	The Return Type Rule refuses the following pair, where trait \TYP{Round}
69	extends trait \TYP{Shape} and object \TYP{Circle} extends \TYP{Round}:
70	%(* Not allowed! *)
71	%ident[\T extends Shape\](x: T): T = x
72	%ident(x: Round): Round = x
73	\begin{Fortress}
74	\(\mathtt{(*}\;\hbox{\rm  Not allowed! \unskip}\;\mathtt{*)}\)\\
75	\(\VAR{ident}\llbracket{}T \KWD{extends} \TYP{Shape}\rrbracket(x\COLON{}T)\COLON{}T = x\)\\
76	\(\VAR{ident}(x\COLON \TYP{Round})\COLON \TYP{Round} = x\)
77	\end{Fortress}
78	The second declaration is more specific than the first.
79	For an argument of type \TYP{Circle}, the instance of the first at \TYP{Circle}
80	is applicable and returns a \TYP{Circle}, but the second, which runs for such an
81	argument, returns only a \TYP{Round}, and it has no other instance.
82	The pair is allowed when the second declaration is itself generic:
83	%ident[\T extends Shape\](x: T): T = x
84	%ident[\T extends Round\](x: T): T = x
85	\begin{Fortress}
86	\(\VAR{ident}\llbracket{}T \KWD{extends} \TYP{Shape}\rrbracket(x\COLON{}T)\COLON{}T = x\)\\
87	\(\VAR{ident}\llbracket{}T \KWD{extends} \TYP{Round}\rrbracket(x\COLON{}T)\COLON{}T = x\)
88	\end{Fortress}
89	Its instance at any type to which it is applicable returns that type.
90	\revision{revival-overloading}{This section is new.
91	The Working Draft of February 2011 required overloaded declarations to have
92	identical static parameters and ignored them.
93	The rules stated are those of the type group's paper of 2011 (Allen, Hilburn,
94	Kilpatrick, Luchangco, Ryu, Chase, and Steele, ``Type Checking Modular Multiple
95	Dispatch with Parametric Polymorphism and Multiple Inheritance'', OOPSLA 2011),
96	which the compiled type checker applies, with its Return Type Rule checked over
97	every instance of the less specific declaration; the pair refused above has the
98	shape of that paper's own example, which the compiled path used to accept and
99	then fail to verify.
100	The interpreter runs the pair allowed above, reading two declarations with
101	static parameters on their declared domains, each over its own instances, where
102	no pair of their parameter types excludes each other; the compiled code
103	generator fails on it (row~537 of the revival's gap ledger).
104	The compiled implementation adds one restriction that is not a rule of the
105	language.
106	When one declaration with static parameters is more specific than another that
107	declares as many static parameters of its own, the two must declare them of the
108	same kinds, position by position, and the return type of the more specific one
109	must be a subtype of the other's when their static parameters are identified
110	position by position; for its code generator hands the static arguments that a call writes,
111	by position, to whichever declaration the call dispatches to (row~398 of the
112	revival's gap ledger).
113	So it refuses this override, whose return type names its static parameters in
114	the other order, where \TYP{Pr} is an object with two type parameters:
115	%trait Maker
116	%  make[\A, B\](): Pr[\A, B\]
117	%end
118	%object M extends Maker
119	%  make[\A, B\](): Pr[\B, A\] = Pr[\B, A\](1)
120	%end
121	\begin{Fortress}
122	\(\KWD{trait} \TYP{Maker}\)\\
123	{\tt~~}\pushtabs\=\+\(  \VAR{make}\llbracket{}A, B\rrbracket()\COLON \TYP{Pr}\llbracket{}A, B\rrbracket\)\-\\\poptabs
124	\(\KWD{end}\)\\
125	\(\KWD{object} M \KWD{extends} \TYP{Maker}\)\\
126	{\tt~~}\pushtabs\=\+\(  \VAR{make}\llbracket{}A, B\rrbracket()\COLON \TYP{Pr}\llbracket{}B, A\rrbracket = \TYP{Pr}\llbracket{}B, A\rrbracket(1)\)\-\\\poptabs
127	\(\KWD{end}\)
128	\end{Fortress}
129	The type group's paper of 2019 (Park, Hong, Steele and Ryu, POPL 2019)
130	instantiates a dispatched declaration from the dynamic types of the arguments
131	and the static type of the call, renaming static parameters freely
132	(\secref{resolving-overloading}); under it the override is valid, and the
133	restriction goes when the compiled dispatcher instantiates a declaration that
134	way.}
